      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "MUNI streetcar service opened, turning San Francisco’s last great wilderness into a residential neighborhood.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.foundsf.org/West_Portal",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "West Portal sits between Portola, Kensington, Taraval, and 15th Avenue, once sand dunes and vegetable farms before development.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.foundsf.org/West_Portal",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "location_description"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "High‑quality homes on detached lots spurred rapid growth in the 1920s, making West Portal the commercial and transportation hub for the West of Twin Peaks.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.foundsf.org/West_Portal",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The area that became West Portal was part of Rancho San Miguel, granted in 1846, and remained a ranch until well after the 20th century.",
